---
/**
 * foodcraft / AboutBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar" (BO), the OUR STORY section.
 *
 * ROLE: the tea bar talking about itself. The design draws a two-column split —
 * PHOTOGRAPH ON THE LEFT on the lilac wash, the story on the RIGHT: a lilac pill
 * eyebrow, a Baloo 2 headline carrying its purple colour-emphasis, the owner's
 * paragraphs, then a wrapping row of white PILL SPEC CHIPS, each one a label
 * with its value set in the purple. Nothing in it has a sharp corner.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="ABOUT" in "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html":
 *   grid        minmax(0,.9fr) minmax(0,1.1fr), gap 52px, align-items center
 *   photo       LEFT column · height 400px · radius 28px · ground #F0E4F6
 *   eyebrow     inline-block PILL · 11px / 700 / .14em / uppercase ·
 *               colour #5C3580 on a #EEE2F5 ground · padding 8px 16px ·
 *               radius 100px                          → the wrapper's .kick
 *   headline    Baloo 2 700 clamp(30px,4vw,52px)/1.08, margin .24em 0 .3em,
 *               #241A22, with a #7B4FA8 <span> emphasis
 *                                                     → the wrapper's .h2
 *   paragraphs  16.5px / 1.82 / #5D5158 / max-width 50ch / margin 0 0 1em,
 *               the LAST one 0 0 26px (the gap above the spec chips)
 *   spec row    flex · wrap · gap 10px
 *   spec chip   #FFFFFF ground · radius 100px · padding 11px 18px · 13px ·
 *               600 · #241A22, with the value in a 700 #7B4FA8 strong
 * The photograph stays on the LEFT and the chips keep their wrapping flow.
 * Neither has been moved to suit an editor mechanism.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S OWN. An audit had to rename a whole set of
 * classes copied verbatim from a sibling, so nothing here reuses AboutBM's
 * `.tah-about-*`: every scoped class is prefixed `.sip-about-` for Sipsip. Only
 * the HOUSE STYLE crossed over — the doc block, the tolerant reads, the guard
 * discipline. No palette, no layout and no class name did.
 *
 * R1 · NO LITERAL HEX. Every hex the design writes here is used through the
 * PageBO token that holds the same value:
 *   #EEE2F5 --lilac · #5C3580 --accent-deep · #241A22 --ink · #5D5158 --ink-soft
 *   #FFFFFF --card · #7B4FA8 --accent · 100px --r-pill
 * .plate supplies the photo ground and its corner, .kick the eyebrow type and
 * .h2 the headline, so the only values restated below are the ones no primitive
 * knows: the two grid tracks, the 400px plate, the paragraph's 16.5/1.82/50ch,
 * the 26px gap, the eyebrow's pill ground and the two chip cell types.
 *   ONE COLOUR HAS NO EXACT TOKEN and is reported rather than invented: the
 *   design's first photo ground is #F0E4F6, and no PageBO token holds that
 *   value. The nearest is --well / --lilac (#EEE2F5, each channel within 2/255),
 *   which is what .plate already paints, so the plate takes the token and no hex
 *   is written. Flagged so nobody reads it as a miss.
 *   THE PAIRING RISK IN THIS PALETTE DOES NOT ARISE HERE: this section sits on
 *   the plain --paper ground and uses neither .band-pink nor .band-sage, so no
 *   purple is ever set on the pink. The one pink token this section's design
 *   touches (--pink-2, the second photo's ground) belongs to an element that is
 *   not rendered — see DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (each one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `about` group, so every data-field below is a hook a real sidebar
 * input owns):
 *   about.image             (data-image-field) — the left photograph
 *   about.tag               — eyebrow (.kick)
 *   about.headline          — h2, set:html so an owner's own <em> lands wherever
 *                             THEY put it in THEIR sentence; the design's purple
 *                             emphasis is PageBO's global .h2 em rule, not
 *                             anything written here
 *   about.lead              — the FIRST paragraph. Rendered on purpose: the
 *                             sidebar's "Lead paragraph" box writes about.lead,
 *                             and a schema field no component renders is the
 *                             mirror image of a dead hook and just as broken.
 *   about.paragraphs.${i}   — the remaining paragraphs (set:html)
 *   about.specs.${i}.label  — the chip's label cell
 *   about.specs.${i}.value  — the chip's purple value cell
 * about.signature is READ once — as the photograph's accessible name — and is
 * never printed. See R9.
 *
 * R2 · NOTHING VISIBLE IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word. The design's demo
 * copy ("Our story", "Real tea, real milk, pearls cooked hourly.", the six
 * sample spec rows) is the DESIGNER filling their own template, and the image
 * slot's "The counter" is an editor affordance, not page copy — none of it
 * ships. A shop that wrote no spec rows advertises none.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT and
 * not just its text: no empty eyebrow pill, no headline over nothing, no empty
 * plate, and — because a spec chip IS its own white pill — no pill printed
 * around a row that is not there.
 * NO STAR ROW exists in this section: the design's ABOUT carries no rating and
 * the schema exposes no numeric rating on about.*, so there is nothing here that
 * could print five stars for a customer who gave no number.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never invented):
 *   · paraRows / specRows drop blank entries but carry the ORIGINAL array index
 *     into data-field (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into
 *     row 3. Nothing decorative is numbered in this section — the chips carry no
 *     numeral and no rail index — so no display index is needed.
 *   · a spec row survives on EITHER cell — a label the owner typed but has not
 *     filled in yet is still their line; only a wholly empty row is dropped.
 *   · about.paragraphs / about.specs are read tolerantly (array or bare string;
 *     objects or bare strings inside). Draft content has arrived as both shapes
 *     before, and a `.map is not a function` costs the whole page, not one row.
 *   · the grid drops to ONE track whenever only one column has content, so a
 *     missing photo never leaves a 400px hole beside the copy. Above 900px the
 *     surviving column then keeps the MEASURE the design gives it — 631px of
 *     copy, 517px of photo, which ARE this grid's two tracks inside PageBO's
 *     1200px .wrap (1200 − 52px gap, split 1.1fr / .9fr) — rather than
 *     stretching across the page. This is the common case, not an edge one:
 *     derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image, so a copy-only ABOUT is what
 *     a first build renders.
 *   · the image url has its apostrophes percent-escaped before it goes into
 *     url('…'), so an upload named "sipsip's counter.jpg" cannot close the quote
 *     early and take the whole declaration down with it.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5): tag and headline are deliberately NOT counted.
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets about.tag = 'About' on EVERY site and
 * about.headline = `About ${name}` on every named one, so a gate that counted
 * them would be always-true and would ship a heading over an empty section. The
 * gate counts BODY content only: the lead, the surviving paragraphs, the
 * surviving spec rows, the photograph. (about.lead is also derived, but from the
 * owner's OWN facts — their name, city and business type — so it is a real
 * sentence about a real business, which is what body content means here.)
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight:
 *   · THE SECOND PHOTOGRAPH. The design stacks a smaller plate under the first
 *     one — height 230px, width 66%, margin -52px 0 0 auto, a --pink-2 ground
 *     inside a 6px --paper border. It is not drawn here because the `about`
 *     group in genericContentSchema.ts declares exactly ONE image, about.image.
 *     A second plate could only be (a) a data-field on an undeclared path such
 *     as about.image2 — a dead hook no sidebar input owns, which is what an
 *     older template in this family shipped — or (b) an empty pink rectangle
 *     with no upload behind it, which is furniture this component invented. Both
 *     are worse than its absence, so the design's PRIMARY plate is rendered at
 *     its own 400px and the overlap is left out. If about.image2 is ever
 *     declared, the second plate belongs back here exactly as specified above.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7). This section renders no href of any kind —
 *     the design's ABOUT has no button, no link and no action, so there is no
 *     data-href-field to remap, and a helper that is never called is dead code
 *     that later drifts out of step with the wrapper. If a link is ever added
 *     here it must arrive WITH the helper copied in from a sibling, and for THIS
 *     page that means PAGE_ANCHORS = menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main
 *     with ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location + contact → visit, services + rooms →
 *     menu, host + why → about, work → gallery, book → main, and off-page hrefs
 *     (tel: mailto: http) passing through untouched. The anchor duty this file
 *     DOES own is its id="about" — one of PageBO's six page anchors, and the
 *     target the header nav and the host/why aliases resolve to.
 *   · about.signature and about.role as VISIBLE text. They belong to a host
 *     identity row, which is a different design; the ABOUT element here draws a
 *     photograph, a story and a chip row, and no person.
 *   · a .cap pill on the plate. The design's about photograph carries no caption
 *     and no schema field would let an owner write one, so a caption here could
 *     only be a literal this file invented.
 *   · .band-pink / .band-sage / .band-lilac. The design draws ABOUT on the plain
 *     blush ground; the pink and sage grounds are spent elsewhere on this page.
 *   · the design's own headline font-size and line-height (clamp(30px,4vw,52px)
 *     / 1.08). PageBO's shared .h2 is clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px) / 1.12 — the same
 *     52px cap, a 2px lower floor and a slightly looser leading — and forking a
 *     font-size into one of fifteen sections would leave this headline out of
 *     step with the other fourteen. The wrapper normalised it; the wrapper keeps
 *     it. Only the design's MARGINS are set here.
 *   · the design's 28px / 24px plate corners. PageBO already normalised this
 *     design's 22–28px card radii into one --r-lg (26px), which is what .plate
 *     paints. Writing 28px here would make this the only section in the template
 *     off the token.
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `padding:76px 28px 0`: the 1200/gutter half is the shared .wrap, and the top
 * is `html[data-page="BO"] section`'s clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px), which caps 4px
 * above the design's 76px. The design's bottom-0 is a WHOLE-PAGE rhythm decision
 * (every unbanded section in it pads top only) and belongs in the wrapper's
 * shared rule; one section zeroing its own bottom while fourteen siblings keep
 * theirs is exactly the local re-derivation that puts a template out of step
 * with its design. Flagged, not done here.
 *
 * R9 · the plate renders ONLY when there is a real upload, so role="img" is
 * honest by construction. Its accessible name is about.signature — the owner's
 * OWN words (derive-content-defaults seeds it from the business name, so on a
 * tea bar the photograph announces as the tea bar). Never a literal written
 * here. With no signature the photo is HIDDEN from the accessibility tree rather
 * than announced as something we guessed, and that is done with ONE conditional
 * aria-hidden — never a literal aria-hidden="true" sitting beside a conditional
 * one, which is the duplicated-attribute bug earlier components shipped: the
 * literal wins and hides content that should have been announced. Nothing else
 * in this section is decorative — there is no numeral and no icon to hide.
 *
 * R3 · one scoped style block, no is:global. Astro's scoped styles cannot reach
 * elements created by set:html, so the owner's own <em> inside the headline is
 * styled by PageBO's global `:where(.h2 em)` rule (font-style normal, --accent —
 * a COLOUR change, because Baloo 2 has no italic and asking for one synthesises
 * an oblique that fights its rounded terminals) and NOT from here. Anything this
 * file ever needed to say about an owner <em> would have to go through
 * :global().
 *
 * R4 · this section has no buttons at all, so none of .btn--fill / .btn--pink /
 * .btn--cream / .btn--line appears; nor does any engine-pinned name
 * (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi, or `.hero` on a
 * <section>).
 *
 * R8 · every clamp this file owns was traced ACROSS the breakpoints, because two
 * audits found type and plates that GREW as the viewport narrowed:
 *   plate height  400px above 900 · clamp(260px,44vw,360px) from 900 down
 *                 (44vw = 396 at 900, so it enters at its 360px cap, below 400)
 *                 · clamp(200px,50vw,260px) from 520 down. At 520px the first
 *                 yields 44vw = 228.8 clamped UP to its 260px floor and the
 *                 second yields 50vw = 260 clamped DOWN to its 260px cap — they
 *                 meet at exactly 260px, and the curve only ever falls.
 *   grid gap      52px above 900 · clamp(28px,4.4vw,44px) below it, which is
 *                 39.6px at the 900 boundary and falls from there.
 *   paragraph     a flat 16.5px at every width.
 *   eyebrow       no font-size is set here at all, so the wrapper's 11px → 10.5px
 *                 step at 520 is the whole curve. The .14em tracking this file
 *                 sets is the design's, and it is the same value the wrapper
 *                 drops to at 520, so nothing moves.
 * At 360px nothing may push sideways: the eyebrow, headline, paragraphs and both
 * chip cells all take overflow-wrap: anywhere. There is no button in this
 * section, so the full-width `white-space: normal` + `overflow-wrap: anywhere`
 * pair has nothing to apply to.
 *
 * CONTRAST, COMPUTED from PageBO's own token hexes (sRGB relative luminance,
 * WCAG 2.x) rather than copied from its table — the method was checked against
 * two of the wrapper's own figures first and reproduces BOTH exactly, with no
 * disagreement to explain away (ink/paper 15.92, ink-soft/paper 7.13):
 *   eyebrow      --accent-deep on --lilac   7.40:1
 *   headline     --ink on --paper          15.92:1
 *   paragraphs   --ink-soft on --paper      7.13:1
 *   chip label   --ink on --card           16.86:1
 *   chip value   --accent on --card         5.96:1
 * Nothing in this section is below AA, and no pair here puts the purple on the
 * pink — the one combination this palette cannot survive.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBO passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;
const about = content.about || {};

const tag = about.tag || '';
const headline = about.headline || '';
const lead = about.lead || '';
const image = String(about.image || '');
// An apostrophe in an upload's filename would otherwise close the url('') early
// and take the whole background-image declaration down with it.
const imageCss = image.replace(/'/g, '%27');

// Read tolerantly: a draft has arrived with this as a bare string before, and a
// `.map is not a function` costs the whole page rather than one paragraph. The
// only fallback taken is the SAME path as a string — folding in a legacy
// `about.body` is not done here, because the data-field would then point at
// about.paragraphs.0 while the text lived somewhere else, and an input that
// edits a different string than the one under it is a broken hook.
const paragraphSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.paragraphs)
  ? about.paragraphs
  : (typeof about.paragraphs === 'string' && about.paragraphs.trim()
    ? [about.paragraphs]
    : []);
// A blank row in the admin's paragraph list would otherwise render an empty <p>
// that still spends its margin. Drop the blanks, carry the ORIGINAL index (R6).
const paraRows = paragraphSource
  .map((text: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(text ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((row) => row.text.trim().length > 0);

// Same discipline for the chips. An item may legitimately be an object
// {label,value} (what the schema's newItem creates) or, from an older draft, a
// bare string — treated as a label with no value rather than dropped.
const specSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.specs) ? about.specs : [];
const specRows = specSource
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    const item = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { label: row, value: '' };
    return {
      label: String(item.label ?? '').trim(),
      value: String(item.value ?? '').trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  // EITHER cell keeps the row. Only a wholly empty row goes, so no white pill is
  // ever drawn around a row that is not there.
  .filter((row) => row.label.length > 0 || row.value.length > 0);

// The photograph's accessible name — the owner's own words, never a literal.
const photoName = String(about.signature || '').trim();

// Track count, computed here so the published grid never keeps an empty 400px
// column standing beside a single populated one.
const hasCopy = Boolean(tag || headline || lead) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
const hasPhoto = Boolean(image);
// R5 — BODY content only. tag and headline are excluded: both are derived onto
// every site, so counting them would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = Boolean(lead || image) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sip-about" id="about">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class:list={['sip-about-grid', !(hasCopy && hasPhoto) && 'sip-about-grid--solo']}>
        {hasPhoto && (
          /* LEFT column, as the design draws it. .plate already gives the lilac
             ground, the design's generous --r-lg corner and the overflow clip. */
          <div class="plate sip-about-plate reveal">
            <div
              class="ph"
              data-image-field="about.image"
              role={photoName ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={photoName || undefined}
              aria-hidden={photoName ? undefined : 'true'}
              style={`background-image:url('${imageCss}');`}
            ></div>
          </div>
        )}
        {hasCopy && (
          <div class="sip-about-copy">
            {tag && <span class="kick sip-about-kick" data-field="about.tag">{tag}</span>}
            {headline && <h2 class="h2 sip-about-h reveal" data-field="about.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
            {/* about.lead comes from a plain textarea, so it is bound as TEXT.
                The paragraph LIST below is where an owner writes markup. */}
            {lead && <p class="lede sip-about-p reveal" data-delay="1" data-field="about.lead">{lead}</p>}
            {paraRows.map((row, k: number) => (
              <p
                class="lede sip-about-p reveal"
                data-delay={String((k % 3) + 1)}
                data-field={`about.paragraphs.${row.i}`}
                set:html={row.text}
              ></p>
            ))}
            {specRows.length > 0 && (
              <div class="sip-about-specs reveal" data-delay="2">
                {specRows.map((row) => (
                  /* One white pill per row, exactly as the design draws it. Each
                     cell is guarded on its own, so a half-filled row prints one
                     cell and not one cell plus a gap. */
                  <span class="sip-about-spec">
                    {row.label && (
                      <span class="sip-about-l" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.label`}>{row.label}</span>
                    )}
                    {row.value && (
                      <strong class="sip-about-v" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.value`}>{row.value}</strong>
                    )}
                  </span>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* THE DESIGN'S SPLIT, verbatim: .9fr of photograph against 1.1fr of story,
     52px apart, the two columns centred on each other. The photo leads because
     the design puts it first; that order is also the DOM order, so the single
     column below 900px reads photograph-then-story exactly as the design does. */
  .sip-about-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: 52px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .sip-about-grid--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .sip-about-copy { min-width: 0; }

  /* The design has no one-column ABOUT, so --solo is a state this file has to
     answer for. The family answer is to keep the surviving column at the MEASURE
     it has in the design rather than let it stretch across the whole wrap. It
     matters most here: a copy-only ABOUT is the DEFAULT first build —
     derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image. 631px and 517px ARE this
     grid's two tracks inside PageBO's 1200px .wrap (1200 − 52px gap, split
     1.1fr / .9fr), so this restores the design's proportions rather than
     inventing new ones. Below 900px one track is everybody's layout and the caps
     lift. */
  @media (min-width: 901px) {
    .sip-about-grid--solo .sip-about-copy { max-width: 631px; }
    .sip-about-grid--solo .sip-about-plate { max-width: 517px; }
  }

  /* THE EYEBROW. The wrapper's .kick already carries the design's type — 11px,
     700, uppercase, inline-block — and the wrapper owns the whole size curve
     (it steps to 10.5px at 520), so no font-size is written here. What .kick
     does NOT know is that in THIS design the eyebrow is a PILL: a lilac ground,
     the deep purple label, 8px/16px of padding and the 100px corner. Those four
     and the design's .14em tracking (the wrapper's base is .18em) are the only
     things restated. The label is --accent-deep and not --accent because that is
     what the design writes; it also happens to be the darker of the two, which
     this ground can afford. max-width + overflow-wrap keep one long owner word —
     a hyphenless place name — from pushing the document sideways at 360px (R8).
     Everything here is qualified with .sip-about-copy so it out-specifies the
     wrapper's :where() primitive whatever order the sheets land in. */
  .sip-about-copy .sip-about-kick {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    background-color: var(--lilac);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The headline is the wrapper's .h2 (Baloo 2 700, clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px)/1.12,
     --ink); only the design's own margins are set here, and no font-size — the
     shared clamp and the shared 760 pass own the whole size curve. The purple
     emphasis is PageBO's global rule: a scoped rule could not reach markup
     created by set:html (R3). The wrapper's shared 22ch cap is left in place;
     against this column's 631px it sits near the same measure, so at most it
     wraps a long headline a word earlier — the design constrains this headline
     with the COLUMN, and the 760 pass releases the cap entirely. */
  .sip-about-copy .sip-about-h { margin: .24em 0 .3em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* THE PARAGRAPH. The wrapper's .lede already carries the family and the
     design's --ink-soft; the three metrics the design states for THIS section —
     16.5px, 1.82, 50ch — are not .lede's defaults (15.5–17.5px / 1.72 / 46ch),
     so they are set here rather than by bending the shared primitive that other
     sections also use. PageBO's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` carries the
     wrapper's own element specificity, hence the parent-class qualifier. */
  .sip-about-copy .sip-about-p {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.82;
    max-width: 50ch;
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design gives its LAST paragraph `margin:0 0 26px` — the gap above the
     chips. That gap is spent by .sip-about-specs below instead, so the same 26px
     appears in the same place while a section with NO chips ends flush rather
     than trailing 26px of nothing. */
  .sip-about-copy .sip-about-p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }
  /* A cleared headline is a real case, and then the eyebrow pill would sit flush
     on the first paragraph — the h2's .24em top margin is what normally opens
     that gap. Adjacency, so it fires only in that case, and qualified because
     the shorthand above would otherwise re-zero the top margin. */
  .sip-about-copy .sip-about-kick + .sip-about-p { margin-top: 14px; }

  /* THE SPEC CHIPS — a wrapping row, 10px apart. This is NOT the wrapper's
     .chip: that primitive is the lilac/purple label this design uses for tags,
     while the spec chip is a WHITE pill with an ink label, so it is written out
     rather than fought with four overrides. */
  .sip-about-specs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-top: 26px;
  }
  /* The pill itself. The design separates its two cells with a literal space in
     the markup; an inline-flex gap of .3em puts the same space in the same place
     while letting each cell be guarded independently — a value-only row prints
     no leading space, and a label-only row prints no trailing one. The inner
     wrap plus overflow-wrap is what keeps a long owner value inside the pill at
     360px instead of widening the document (R8).
     LINE-HEIGHT IS DECLARED, and it is the one metric on this pill the design
     does not write: its chip inherits the demo page's body, which sets none, so
     it renders at the browser normal (~1.36 for Nunito Sans). PageBO's body
     sets 1.72, and inheriting that would add ~4.6px to every pill — the same
     inflation the wrapper's .btn declares line-height:1 to avoid. 1 is not
     available here: a .btn never wraps and this pill does at 360px, where 1
     would collide the two lines. 1.5 is the tightest value that still opens a
     wrapped pill, and it leaves a one-line pill ~1.8px taller than the design's
     rather than ~4.6px. */
  .sip-about-spec {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 .3em;
    max-width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--card);
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    padding: 11px 18px;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The label inherits the pill's 13px/600/--ink; only the wrap guard is its
     own. The value is the design's purple 700 — the one place in this section
     the brand colour appears as text, and it appears on the white card, never
     on a pink. */
